verdict.why:
I could not refute the finding. The pre-registration says "teacher_ceiling.json says ok" and lists "the teacher's greedy decode gives the stored continuation" among the things ok certifies. The code does not do that. In eval/teacher_ceiling.py, lines 357-359 set ok = student_prompts_rebuilt AND forwards_agree (|base_ce − base_stock_ce| ≤ 2e-3) AND teacher_reproduces_itself (teacher_near ≥ 0.97) AND, where it exists, evaluator.reproduced. Lines 306-311 append the greedy re-decode to checks.greedy_decode and nothing else reads it. Downstream nothing reads it either: results/constitution/arms_controls.py uses only bool(ceil.get("ok")) (lines 137 and 296-297), and the resume test in the chain (tempcontrol_qwen0.5b.sh line 67) checks only d['ok'] and n==100.

The identity part is harmless. A failed identity check raises SystemExit (lines 264-267), no json is written, and the chain stops at "CEILING FAILED". So whenever a teacher_ceiling.json exists, identity has passed. The only real mismatch is the greedy decode.

Would the rule mislead? Only if a probe re-decode diverges, and that is unlikely:
- the re-decode uses the builder's own cached, deepcopy-cloned path;
- the 0.5B stats record a cache_check of 3/3 same and 0 uncached fallbacks;
- mlx 0.32.2 was installed on Aug 28, before the data build on Sep 12;
- every 9B and Bonsai greedy_decode entry is null.

The finding's evidence also overstates the risk. The divergence at token 48 in the builder's docstring was cached against uncached, which is not what this re-decode tests. Still, if a divergence happens, the rule can be read two ways, and the effect would be large. The teacher is the stock backbone, the same for every arm, on the same first two items of each split. A divergence would therefore show up identically in all nine arms. Read literally, the rule would then void the whole run, while the code and chain report every arm. That is exactly the kind of fork the "no_reading_is_changed_after_the_runs" clause is meant to prevent, so the passage should be fixed before commit.
